<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Denkyira</h1><p class="st-meta">1620 CE – 1701 CE (approx.) · capital Abankeseso</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Akan kingdom that dominated the gold-rich central forest in the 17th century before being overthrown by the rising Asante.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Denkyira emerged in the early 17th century in the forest south-west of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>, among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> clans that had moved from the area of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-adansi">Adansi</a>. Controlling rich goldfields and routes to the coastal forts, it conquered neighbouring states, including Adansi, and grew into the leading power of the central Akan forest.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boa-amponsem">Boa Amponsem</a> in the late 17th century, Denkyira took tribute from many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> states, including the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> chiefdoms, and held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina-note">Elmina Note</a>, a rent paid by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina">Elmina</a>. Its wealth came from gold mining and trade in gold and slaves with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-gold-coast">Dutch Gold Coast</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Denkyira's demands provoked revolt. In 1701 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> defeated and killed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ntim-gyakari">Ntim Gyakari</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-feyiase">Feyiase</a>, and Denkyira became tributary to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a>. It survives as a traditional area in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana's</a> Central Region.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/denkyira.jpg" alt="Denkyira" width="320" height="318"><figcaption>Image: HetmanTheResearcher · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denkyrian_Empire_1700ce.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1620 CE – 1701 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Abankeseso</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Boa Amponsem, Ntim Gyakari</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Akan religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Twi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ghana</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Denkyira, Denkyera</dd></dl></aside></div>
